After completing the inductive construction, we obtain the infinite set $A=\{a_n\}_{n\in\omega}$ with $\sup_{a\in A}\mathsf{dist}(a,C)=\infty$ which is hidden behind $C_0$. This means that the set $C_0$ is $C$-infinitely hiding.

}\label{pf:main}

To prove the first part of Theorem~\ref{main}, for every closed convex subset $C$ of a Banach space $X$  we should prove the equivalence of the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $C$ is approximatively polyhedral;
\item the characteristic cone $V_C$ of $C$ is polyhedral in $X$ and $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,V_C)<\infty$;
\item  $\mathcal H_C$ contains a polyhedral closed convex set;
\item  $\mathcal H_C$ contains no positively hiding closed convex set;
\item the space $\mathcal H_C$ is separable;
\item the space $\mathcal H_C$ has density $\mathrm{dens}(\mathcal H_C)<\mathfrak c$.
\end{enumerate}

It suffices to prove the implications $(1)\Rightarrow(2)\Rightarrow(3)\Rightarrow(5)\Rightarrow(6)\Rightarrow(4)\Rightarrow(2)\Rightarrow(3)\Rightarrow(1)$, among which $(2)\Rightarrow(3)$ and $(5)\Rightarrow(6)$ are trivial. The remaining implications can be established as follows.
\smallskip

To prove the implication $(1)\Rightarrow(2)$, assume that the closed convex set $C$ is approximatively polyhedral and find a polyhedral convex set $P$ with $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,P)<\infty$. Lemma~\ref{l2.4n} implies that $V_C=V_P$. The polyhedral convex set $P$ can be written as a finite intersection $P=\bigcap_{i=1}^n f_i^{-1}\big((-\infty,a_i]\big)$ of closed hyperplanes determined by some functionals $f_1,\dots,f_n:X\to\mathbb R$ and some real numbers $a_1,\dots,a_n\in\mathbb R$. It is easy to check that $$V_C=V_P=\bigcap_{i=1}^n f_i^{-1}\big((-\infty,0]\big),$$
which means that the characteristic cone $V_C$ of $C$ is polyhedral. By Lemma~\ref{l2.6}, $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(P,V_C)=\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(P,V_P)<\infty$. Consequently, $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,V_C)\le \mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,P)+\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(P,V_C)<\infty$.
\smallskip

The implications $(3)\Rightarrow(5)$ and $(3)\Rightarrow(1)$ are proved in Lemma~\ref{l3.4} and $(6)\Rightarrow(4)$ in Lemmas~\ref{l4.2}. The implication $(4)\Rightarrow(2)$ follows from Lemmas~\ref{l5.3}, \ref{l6.3} and \ref{l6.4}.

Next, assuming the Banach space is finite-dimensional we will check that the conditions (1)--(6) are equivalent to:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(7)] $C$ is not positively hiding;
\item[(8)] $C$ is not infinitely hiding.
\end{enumerate}

It suffices to check that $(4)\Rightarrow(7)\Rightarrow(8)\Rightarrow (2)$. In fact, the first two implications $(4)\Rightarrow(7)\Rightarrow(8)$ are trivial, while $(8)\Rightarrow(2)$ follows from Lemmas~\ref{l5.3} and \ref{l5.8}.

A convex subset $C$ of a Banach space $X$ is called
\begin{itemize}
\item {\em a closed half-space} if $C=f^{-1}([a,+\infty)$ for  some non-zero linear continuous functional $f:X\to\mathbb R$ and some real number $a\in\mathbb R$;
\item {\em polyhedral} if $C$ can be written as the intersection $C=\cap \mathcal F$ of a finite family $\mathcal F$ of closed half-spaces in $X$;
\item {\em approximatively polyhedral} if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a closed polyhedral subset $P\subset X$ that lies on  Hausdorff distance $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,P)<\varepsilon$ from $C$.

